WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.392 --> 00:00:13.850
JOHN BARRUS: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.170
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:18.290
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.920
My name is John Barrus, and I'll
be talking about Cloud TPUs.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.165
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform,

00:00:24.165 --> 00:00:26.990
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.080
So today I'll be
joined by Jack--

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.280
I can't even talk--

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.340
Zak Stone, the product manager
at TensorFlow and Cloud TPUs

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.590
from the Google Brain team.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:39.630
So I want to talk a little
bit about machine learning

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.720
and Cloud TPUs and
new opportunities

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.620
that are available now due
to machine learning, and also

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.600
the central computational
challenge related

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.090
to machine learning and
what we're doing about it.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:52.910
So I have a couple of examples
I'd like to mention first.

00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:57.410
You know, digital cameras were
created 40 to 45 years ago,

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.660
and then about 20 years ago
they became popular even

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.900
for consumers.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.080
And if you had a digital
camera 20 years ago,

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.780
you might have been taking
pictures, taking the memory

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.240
card out of the camera and
sticking it into your computer,

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.800
and just saving the
pictures on your hard drive.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.310
And if you wanted to find
a picture from an event

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:16.070
or of a particular person,
you had to kind of either

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.470
have good organizational
skills of how

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.201
to keep track of photos
on your computer,

00:01:20.201 --> 00:01:21.950
or you could remember
when the picture was

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.950
taken, kind of search by date
and try to find that photo.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.020
Well, things have
changed a lot nowadays.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.660
I keep my pictures
on Google Photos.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.290
And if I want to
find, for instance,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.830
a picture of the Golden Gate
Bridge or the Eiffel Tower,

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.170
I can just type in Golden
Gate Bridge or Eiffel Tower

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.490
into the search bar.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:42.050
And because Google Photos has
used machine learning tools

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.212
to identify objects
in those photos,

00:01:44.212 --> 00:01:45.920
it can call up all
the pictures that I've

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.170
taken of the Golden Gate
Bridge or the Eiffel Tower

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.120
and present them
to me so that I can

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.440
go through them very quickly.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.480
It's made my life
so much easier.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.900
In fact, if have a friend that
I'd like to find a picture of,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.390
if I've already tagged
that friend in one photo,

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.100
I can easily find that friend
and many other photos as well.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:03.410
So that's super convenient.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.190
So that's an example of
how consumers can save time

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.759
using machine learning.

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.810
But perhaps you'd be interested
in another example that

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.280
is good for enterprises.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.640
And I'm thinking about
automobile manufacturing lines.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.890
It turns out that when an
automobile manufacturing

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.860
line goes down, it
costs about $22,000

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.680
a minute in lost
revenue, or in costs.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:26.900
And that's up more than
$1 million an hour.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.380
And so having a machine fail
on that manufacturing line

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.370
is actually quite expensive.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:34.760
And so it's much
better if the company

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:38.600
can identify when the
machine might fail, and then

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.700
schedule a maintenance
or repair to that machine

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.640
during a time when the line is
down already for maintenance.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.730
And so what a company
might do in that case

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:50.570
is fit those machines with
sensors, maybe audio sensors,

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:53.180
pressure, temperature,
vibration, or anything

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.340
like that, and then look at that
data and use machine learning

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.090
and use the computers
to identify patterns

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.910
in that data that might
indicate when a machine might

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.260
be ready for maintenance.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.426
And by doing that, they can
schedule the maintenance

00:03:05.426 --> 00:03:08.480
on the machine instead of
having the machine fail

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.610
at an inopportune time.

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.580
So here's some
other examples, too.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:17.510
For instance, the Google
Arts and Culture app

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:20.900
that runs on your
phone can use a selfie

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:24.110
and identify a
picture or a drawing

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:27.350
by a famous artist that most
closely matches your selfie.

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.490
So our colleague
Sateesh did this here.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.820
And it turns out he
looked most like Chuck

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:33.839
Berry in this drawing
by Gilbert Shelton.

00:03:33.839 --> 00:03:35.630
There's another example
that is very useful

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:36.890
when you're traveling.

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:39.260
If you're in a foreign
country and you

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.840
can't read the language
that's presented on a street

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:44.510
sign or a menu, you can
actually use the camera

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.300
to take a picture of that
menu, and then Google

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.090
uses machine learning
to identify the text,

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.750
convert that text to a
machine readable format,

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.520
convert it into a
language we understand,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.350
and insert that
language in the text

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.470
that we can read in the
same place on the image

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.720
so that we can read a menu
even in a foreign language.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.060
It's incredibly convenient.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.260
Now here's another example
of making life easier

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.370
is Smart Reply in Inbox,
where when a piece of mail

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.650
comes in, instead of having
to type with your thumbs

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.840
the whole response,
Smart Reply offers

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.319
a few responses that might be
appropriate for this message.

00:04:17.319 --> 00:04:19.610
And you can just press a
button, and then that response

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.110
is inserted into the email.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:23.235
Then you can add your own
comments and send it off.

00:04:23.235 --> 00:04:25.910
It's a nice time saver.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.210
Now it turns out that making
all this machine learning work

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:32.480
effectively and quickly
requires a substantial amount

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.970
of computation power
because of the increasing

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:38.120
complexity of machine learning
models that are in use.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.070
So for instance,
image recognition

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:45.160
and object detection models
that are run on images

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.900
often have 50 or 100
or even more layers,

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.270
and billions of
neurons that can be

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:53.580
activated by the information
coming in to the model.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:56.819
So in this example, you can see
the input layer at the bottom.

00:04:56.819 --> 00:04:58.610
Perhaps that input
layer is taking the data

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:00.485
from the machines that
have been instrumented

00:05:00.485 --> 00:05:01.850
in the manufacturing line.

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:05.090
And neurons are activated
based on the input data,

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:08.210
and then that information
activates additional neurons

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.180
in layers above that until
finally, the final output

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.060
layer might only have two
settings, either Needs

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.310
Maintenance Soon or Doesn't
Need Maintenance Soon.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:19.190
And so that's an example
of how the neurons work

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.490
in the machine learning model.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.560
Now what we're finding
is that current solutions

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.860
haven't provided enterprises
with enough computing power.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.640
And recent AI advances are
almost all attributable

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.970
to advances in compute power.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.110
And in fact, we
surveyed attendees

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.690
at a recent TensorFlow event
and found that most of them

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.910
felt like their compute
power was inadequate.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:44.370
They needed much more
computational power.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:48.860
So our mission at Google
is to democratize AI, make

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:52.670
it available both through
services and infrastructure,

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:55.940
making it accessible, fast,
and useful for enterprises

00:05:55.940 --> 00:05:57.530
and developers.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.420
We have over 10,000
customers taking

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:01.370
advantage of our AI solutions.

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.320
We've launched 12
products last year

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.630
with dozens of
features related to AI.

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.562
And we have over 30
Cloud AI partners.

00:06:07.562 --> 00:06:09.020
And in fact, we
have over a million

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:13.980
Kaggle members that are
using AI on our platform.

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.380
So now, here to tell you more
about Cloud TPUs is Zak Stone.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.490
Zak?

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:22.110
ZAK STONE: Thanks, John.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:23.190
Hi, everyone.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.730
I'm really excited to talk to
you today about Cloud TPUs.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:29.540
As you can see here,
this Cloud TPU board

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:32.000
is the latest offering
in Google Cloud,

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.460
and it's Google's
second generation

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.350
machine learning accelerator.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:39.750
So as a Product
Manager for TensorFlow,

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.580
one thing that John
mentioned earlier

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.410
is that most
TensorFlow users who

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.900
are working with state of
the art deep neural networks

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.820
are hungry for more compute.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.280
In TensorFlow it's
open source, it's free,

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.650
it runs across a huge variety
of different platforms

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:56.010
from state of the art
CPUs to GPUs to Cloud TPUs

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.350
and onto mobile and
embedded devices.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.500
So across all those
devices, we're

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.910
committed to
TensorFlow providing

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.196
fantastic performance.

00:07:04.196 --> 00:07:06.570
Today we're focusing on this
Cloud TPU, this new addition

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.730
to the lineup in Google Cloud.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:12.330
So what's special about
this second generation TPU?

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.814
Well, first of all,
on the reference

00:07:14.814 --> 00:07:16.230
workloads that
we've open-sourced,

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.940
this provides differentiated
performance per dollar.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.850
We think this is a
really economical way

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.820
to get work done, especially
on some of these incredibly

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.100
intense computational
challenges associated

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.440
with state of the art
machine learning systems.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.632
Each board delivers
up to 180 teraflops

00:07:30.632 --> 00:07:32.340
per device, which is
a lot if you've ever

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.960
built a system yourself.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.820
And by hosting
these in Cloud, you

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.360
can scale up and scale down
easily as your needs change,

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.080
instead of having to put in the
capital and expertise and data

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:44.640
center cooling and
power and so forth

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.124
to build a cluster like
this and manage it yourself.

00:07:47.124 --> 00:07:49.540
Right now these Cloud TPUs are
architected for TensorFlow,

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:53.010
so you can program them with
TensorFlow's high level APIs

00:07:53.010 --> 00:07:55.560
instead of having to do
bespoke supercomputer

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:59.365
programming as you might
have in a previous era.

00:07:59.365 --> 00:08:00.740
Let me tell you
a little bit more

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.650
about the motivation
behind these Cloud TPUs.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:05.540
First of all, it's
really important

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.680
to be able to train and run
complex machine learning models

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:10.520
faster.

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.020
As John mentioned,
one of the trends

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.900
that we've observed over
the past several decades,

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:17.780
really, but especially
over the past five years

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:21.590
is that increasing computational
power and scale of these models

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:24.230
has led to much higher
accuracy and unlocked

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:26.960
new applications that just
wouldn't have been feasible

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:28.640
previously.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.310
Also, we find that it's
really important here

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:35.419
at Google, and elsewhere
across the world,

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:38.870
to improve the productivity
of machine learning teams.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.419
When you are training a
machine learning model,

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:44.510
it's common to wait
for hours or days,

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.880
or even, in some cases,
weeks for the model

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.430
to converge and be ready
to deploy in production.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.880
That's like waiting weeks
for your code to compile.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.080
And so what we're trying
to do, both with TensorFlow

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:56.780
across all platforms and
then with Cloud TPUs,

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:01.050
is to reduce that time to hours,
and perhaps some day to minutes

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.020
so people can iterate faster
and be more productive.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.110
Finally, as I
mentioned earlier, it's

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:08.780
really valuable to be able
to scale up and down easily

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.392
with on-demand machine learning
infrastructure in the Cloud.

00:09:11.392 --> 00:09:13.100
You don't have to
fight with provisioning

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.440
machines, configuring drivers.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.410
You can just connect to your
virtual machine to a Cloud TPU,

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:20.210
start running open
source TensorFlow,

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:24.000
and train faster
than ever before.

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.890
Let me say a little
bit more about ML

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:30.559
teams of ML engineers, ML
researchers, data scientists.

00:09:30.559 --> 00:09:32.100
One thing that you're
probably aware,

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.010
as we certainly are
here, is that the talent

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:38.100
to build these state of the
art machine learning models

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.890
is in very high demand
right now and very scarce.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.417
And so it's really
important for the people

00:09:43.417 --> 00:09:45.000
that you have in
your organization who

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.280
have the strongest
expertise in this domain

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.720
to be able to move as
quickly as possible.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.200
And so with these
higher performance

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.910
accelerators and great, high
level software on top of them,

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.960
you can enable your teams to
accomplish a lot more than they

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:03.710
could otherwise.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:05.480
Furthermore, I'd
like to emphasize,

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:08.240
as I mentioned before,
that this is now

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.660
the second in a whole line of
machine learning accelerators

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.416
that Google has
developed in-house.

00:10:13.416 --> 00:10:15.290
We had our first generation
tensor processing

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:18.920
unit that was optimized for
inference and used internally.

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:21.560
This is the second
generation tensor processing

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.757
unit built into a
Cloud TPU board,

00:10:23.757 --> 00:10:26.090
and even into larger systems
that I'll tell you about in

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:27.060
a minute.

00:10:27.060 --> 00:10:29.840
So every time you run a
search, your search results

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.050
are ranked in conjunction with
some of these tensor processing

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.370
units here at Google.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:37.190
When you use Google Photos,
as John mentioned earlier,

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.800
some of the organization
of those photos

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.070
is facilitated with these
tensor processing units.

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.170
And across many other
Google products,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.850
you're interacting with
these accelerators every day.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.940
And now you have the
opportunity to incorporate

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.495
the same accelerators
into your own businesses

00:10:52.495 --> 00:10:53.370
through Google Cloud.

00:10:56.276 --> 00:10:57.650
One thing that
we're trying to do

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:01.095
is to make it easy for people
to get started with Cloud TPUs.

00:11:01.095 --> 00:11:02.720
And the way that
we're approaching that

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:05.630
is by open sourcing
reference workloads

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:09.470
that are optimized and ready
to go on these Cloud TPUs.

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:12.110
Initially we have concentrated
on a few categories

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.530
of reference workloads, on
visual recognition, so image

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:16.410
classification.

00:11:16.410 --> 00:11:19.220
These are models like
ResNet-50 or Inception,

00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:22.880
or for lower resource devices,
MobileNet and SqueezeNet.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.720
And this is really useful if
you have an object centered

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:27.470
in a frame, you know
where you're looking,

00:11:27.470 --> 00:11:30.470
and you need to tell is
this a cat or is it a dog?

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:35.180
Is this a vehicle in this
close crop of an image,

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:39.320
or is it pavement for an
autonomous driving application?

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.720
So we have a lot of models
there that are ready to go,

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:43.220
well-optimized, and
we're continuing

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:46.670
to improve their performance
further over time.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:49.030
Secondly, we've got
reference models

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:51.449
in machine translation
and language modeling.

00:11:51.449 --> 00:11:53.740
These are built around the
state of the art transformer

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:56.200
model that's part of the
open-source Tensor2Tensor

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:57.280
library.

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.860
And Transformer has been
really useful for both machine

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.950
translation from one
language to another, also

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.450
for language
modeling, where you're

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:06.610
trying to predict the
next word in a sentence,

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.568
as you might if you're
building a messaging app

00:12:08.568 --> 00:12:11.260
and you want to make it easier
for people to input messages

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:12.700
to one another.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.457
But Transformer is
actually more general

00:12:14.457 --> 00:12:16.540
and useful for other
sequence-to-sequence problems

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.460
as well.

00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:21.820
Finally, related to the small
image visual classification

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:24.100
I mentioned earlier, we've
got a reference model

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.740
for object detection
called RetinaNet.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:29.140
So this is useful if you
don't have a small crop,

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:31.360
but instead you're
searching for small objects

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:33.340
in a large field
of view, like you

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.670
might for autonomous driving,
but also for medical imaging.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:41.050
You might need to look
for regions of an image

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.900
that a medical practitioner
should focus on

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.880
in a large scan that
may be many megapixels.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:49.180
And so RetinaNet might be
a useful starting point

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:52.940
if you're interested in
those sorts of applications.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.680
Just to give you a
sense of how broad

00:12:54.680 --> 00:12:57.452
the application domains are,
even with these building

00:12:57.452 --> 00:12:58.910
blocks of these
reference workloads

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.070
that I mentioned earlier,
I'd like to call out

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:03.670
a few other applications.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:07.010
One that's quite surprising
is genetic variant calling.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:08.930
So when you're
sequencing a genome,

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:10.550
there's this final
step when you're

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.440
reconstructing
the sequence where

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:13.760
you need to call variants.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:15.890
You need to decide,
OK, the reads

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:19.280
are suggesting that there's
a base pair modified here.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:21.500
Is that base pair
really modified or not?

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:23.390
Is this a real variant or not?

00:13:23.390 --> 00:13:26.120
And so a team here at
Google has developed

00:13:26.120 --> 00:13:30.170
an algorithm called Deep Variant
that recasts this problem that

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:33.800
traditionally takes hours
or days on a cluster of CPUs

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.110
as an image recognition
problem that you can now

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:39.050
run on these state of the art
machine learning accelerators,

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:41.210
whether GPUs or TPUs.

00:13:41.210 --> 00:13:45.320
And so this dramatically
speeds up the process,

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:48.020
and we think this will make
genetic sequencing faster

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:51.830
and a lot more affordable
to be used in many new ways.

00:13:51.830 --> 00:13:54.620
As I mentioned before,
autonomous vehicles

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:57.250
are a really important
domain for visual recognition

00:13:57.250 --> 00:13:58.830
and object detection.

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:00.860
And there are many
aspects to that problem.

00:14:00.860 --> 00:14:03.620
There's some teams
that are considering

00:14:03.620 --> 00:14:06.170
running machine
perception on vehicles,

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.260
detecting pedestrians and
street signs and road markings

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:10.850
and obstacles and so forth.

00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:13.070
Those are all visual
perception problems.

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:15.860
But also visual
recognition and processing

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:19.310
is valuable for the mapping
that often goes on beforehand.

00:14:19.310 --> 00:14:21.600
Before the vehicles
even go out on the road,

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.170
you need to understand
the world in great detail

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:27.350
so they have an accurate map
of where they can drive and not

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:28.190
drive.

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:30.410
And finally, obviously,
there's a huge amount

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:31.700
of opportunity in retail.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:33.800
Retailers everywhere
are experimenting

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:37.100
with new kinds of customer
experiences, checkout

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.770
experiences, in-store
experiences, shopping

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:41.180
experiences online.

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:44.060
And all of these experiences
tend to be highly visual

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:47.150
and require detailed
understanding of what's

00:14:47.150 --> 00:14:51.300
present in an image, in a
scene, in a live video stream.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.860
And so we think these state of
the art Cloud TPUs are going

00:14:54.860 --> 00:14:56.730
to be helpful for
rapid prototyping

00:14:56.730 --> 00:15:01.230
and then deployment of these
new retail experiences.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.520
So I mentioned earlier that
these Cloud TPUs on the left

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:08.150
are the ones that are available
today in beta and Google Cloud.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.340
But they're also designed
to be connected together

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.760
into even larger
systems like the TPU pod

00:15:13.760 --> 00:15:15.550
that you can see
over on the right.

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:16.850
Now these pods are amazing.

00:15:16.850 --> 00:15:21.440
This has a total of 256 of
these TPU chips, 64 boards,

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:24.710
and can deliver up to 11 and
1/2 petaflops of computing

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:27.060
performance for a single
machine learning problem,

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:29.090
if you want to train a
model in half an hour

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.620
instead of in days.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:35.250
And these are coming to
Cloud later this year.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.760
We think these
Cloud TPU pods are

00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:39.770
going to provide a
great solution for folks

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.830
who are starting their
development now on Cloud TPUs

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:46.070
with no code changes
to then scale up

00:15:46.070 --> 00:15:49.400
by more than an
order of magnitude

00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:52.490
without any code changes, as
long as you can tolerate larger

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:53.540
batch sizes.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.340
So one thing that we've
announced recently

00:15:55.340 --> 00:15:56.840
is that our own
internal experiments

00:15:56.840 --> 00:15:58.760
have shown that you
can take a model that

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:01.400
would train in
about a day on one

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:04.250
of these boards in a
certain configuration,

00:16:04.250 --> 00:16:06.530
and scale that all the
way down to 30 minutes

00:16:06.530 --> 00:16:08.029
training on a Cloud TPU pod.

00:16:08.029 --> 00:16:10.320
And we're continuing to
optimize these systems further.

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.100
So we're looking forward to
bringing these to Google Cloud

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.050
so you can enjoy them as well.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:19.520
So if you want to get started,
since the demand is intense,

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.560
we ask that you
visit the page here,

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.747
cloud.google.com/tpu
and request quota.

00:16:24.747 --> 00:16:27.080
And we're doing our best to
process those quota requests

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:29.960
as quickly as we can.

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:31.200
Thank you very much.

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:33.350
We'll be taking questions
in just a moment.

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:35.379
We'll be right back
after a minute break.

00:16:35.379 --> 00:16:35.920
Thanks again.

00:17:58.397 --> 00:17:59.480
JOHN BARRUS: Welcome back.

00:17:59.480 --> 00:18:01.313
We have a few questions
we'd like to answer.

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:03.700
So we'll mention the
question and give the answer.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:06.499
First of all, someone said,
I've applied for TPU quota

00:18:06.499 --> 00:18:09.040
during alpha and again at beta,
and I haven't heard anything.

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:11.350
When will I get a chance
to try Cloud TPUs?

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:13.450
Well, I have to say,
they've been very popular.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:15.220
We just ask for your patience.

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.780
You'll get a chance soon.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:19.420
But thanks for your
patience and waiting.

00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:20.340
ZAK STONE: Totally.

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:22.060
Again, we wish we
could give these

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:25.389
to everyone right now, so
we're trying as hard as we can.

00:18:25.389 --> 00:18:26.680
There's a second question here.

00:18:26.680 --> 00:18:29.320
I saw an article indicating
the cost of training on TPUs

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.410
is much more expensive
compared to NVIDIA Volta GPUs.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:35.344
So while we won't make
direct comparisons today,

00:18:35.344 --> 00:18:36.760
one thing that
we'd encourage you,

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:38.650
when you get access
to Cloud TPUs,

00:18:38.650 --> 00:18:41.860
is to run benchmarks yourself
on your own workloads.

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:45.880
It's actually really subtle
to run a benchmark accurately.

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:47.990
You need to decide are
you processing real data

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:49.990
and measuring the full
performance of your input

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:50.870
pipeline?

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:52.294
Are you running
on synthetic data?

00:18:52.294 --> 00:18:54.460
Are you testing that the
model converges all the way

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.280
to the right accuracy, as we do
with our reference workloads?

00:18:57.280 --> 00:19:00.460
Or are you just looking at
the step time performance?

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:01.979
We're pretty confident
that we offer

00:19:01.979 --> 00:19:04.270
differentiated performance
per dollar for our reference

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:05.080
workloads.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:07.720
And we're already starting to
see some of our early users

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:10.460
running their own benchmarks
and talking about them online.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.520
So the performance
will definitely

00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:14.660
be different from one
workload to another.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:16.150
And it's really
important for you

00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:18.430
to run your own
experiments to decide

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:20.920
what accelerator works for you.

00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:24.460
The important thing to recognize
is that Google Cloud's goal

00:19:24.460 --> 00:19:27.580
is to offer the best
experience for machine

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:29.980
learning across all workloads.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:32.410
And that means we're hosting
the state of the art CPUS,

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:35.460
state of the art GPUs, and
state of the art Cloud TPUs.

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:37.960
So we don't want this to be an
either/or or kind of a thing.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:42.490
You should be able to test on
all of the state of the art

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.470
machine learning platforms,
and on a workload

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.136
by workload basis,
figure out which ones

00:19:46.136 --> 00:19:48.661
are most appropriate for you.

00:19:48.661 --> 00:19:51.160
JOHN BARRUS: The next question
is, can I use PyTorch, Caffe,

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:52.618
or other frameworks
for programming

00:19:52.618 --> 00:19:57.754
So today we offer
TensorFlow for program TPUs.

00:19:57.754 --> 00:19:59.920
So that's the only thing
that's available right now.

00:19:59.920 --> 00:20:03.160
We've heard people
request PyTorch and Caffe.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.020
We don't have a
solution for you today,

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:10.689
but I'd say stay in tune, and
if we have anything to offer,

00:20:10.689 --> 00:20:11.480
we'll let you know.

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:13.010
But today it's TensorFlow only.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:14.500
ZAK STONE: Great.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.480
There's a question
here, can I do inference

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:17.780
on TPUs or only training?

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:19.510
So that's a great question.

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:22.690
So TPUs support
training and inference.

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:25.210
At the moment, in
beta, the best support

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:27.730
is, we spend a lot of
time optimizing training.

00:20:27.730 --> 00:20:30.640
We've also got some early
support for batch inference.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.370
If you have some huge
corpus of images, let's say,

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.250
that you need to process with a
trained model, that's something

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:38.000
you can do today.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.420
We are working on more
optimizations over time

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.190
to support low
latency inference,

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:45.964
but we wouldn't recommend
that right now in production.

00:20:45.964 --> 00:20:47.380
JOHN BARRUS: The
next question is,

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:50.475
this is a second generation of
TPU, as Zak mentioned earlier.

00:20:50.475 --> 00:20:51.850
Will there be a
third generation,

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.570
and can you tell us
anything about it?

00:20:53.570 --> 00:20:56.050
Well, Google Cloud has
not announced anything

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:58.540
about future
generations of TPUs.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.210
So we don't have anything
to say about that right now.

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:03.460
ZAK STONE: All I can comment
is that broadly speaking,

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:05.251
hardware architecture
is interesting again.

00:21:05.251 --> 00:21:07.840
So there's a whole variety
of new hardware platforms

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:12.730
that are becoming available
both here and elsewhere.

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:14.492
Let's see if we have
any other questions.

00:21:14.492 --> 00:21:16.450
JOHN BARRUS: We don't
have any other questions.

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:18.580
So stay tuned for
the next session.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:21.814
Showcase your data stories
online with Google Data Studio.

00:21:21.814 --> 00:21:23.230
ZAK STONE: Thanks
for checking in.

00:21:23.230 --> 00:21:25.230
We appreciate it.

